keel-web (0.4.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The Anubis front serves a static file (CSS, JavaScript, an image or a
    font, by a fixed list of extensions) without the challenge, but only
    when it is a file on the disk: try_files, else Anubis. A .php path, a
    path that is not a file and a path with a hidden part go through
    Anubis as before. Anubis answers a request without its cookie with
    the challenge page, status 200, also for a stylesheet (WordPress
    replica of a cloud pair, BR2, 2026-11-07).
  * New directory /etc/nginx/keel-static.d/, which ships empty. The
    application layer sets the root of the static files there, once.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

keel-web (0.4.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * anubis-http.env sets COOKIE_PARTITIONED=false too. Chrome refuses a
    Partitioned cookie without Secure (CHIPS), and Anubis sets Partitioned
    by default: while the certificate was self-signed, a browser never
    passed the challenge and was told "Your browser is configured to
    disable cookies" (commissioning run, BR1 and BR2, 2026-11-06).

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0000

keel-web (0.4.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Port 80 is the application's (handbook decision 0079). While the
    machine's certificate is self-signed, port 80 serves the site as 443
    does, with the routes of keel-default.d and, in the cloud modes,
    through Anubis and Coraza: the Anubis front no longer redirects port
    80 then. When a certificate authority's certificate is in place,
    port 80 redirects to HTTPS, in the simple mode too (a change: before,
    the default site never redirected), except
    /.well-known/acme-challenge/, which is served from dehydrated's
    WELLKNOWN.
  * New /usr/lib/keel-web/http-mode, run by keel-web-http.service at
    boot and at each start of Nginx, and by keel-web-http.path when
    /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/cert.crt changes. It links
    /etc/nginx/keel-web/http-redirect.conf for a CA's certificate, tests
    with nginx -t and reloads, and puts the link back on failure.
  * While the certificate is self-signed, Anubis sets its cookies without
    Secure (COOKIE_SECURE=false, COOKIE_SAME_SITE=Lax in
    /etc/anubis/keel-web-http.env, which a new drop-in of
    anubis@keel.service reads), so a browser on port 80 passes the
    challenge. http-mode restarts a running Anubis when this changes.
  * The loopback addresses of port 80 are the sites' default server, in
    both modes and whatever the certificate: http://127.0.0.1/ reaches the
    application, not the 404 of keel-overlay-nginx's keel-health.conf.
    /keel-health still answers 204 there; behind Anubis it answers the
    loopback only.
  * The content of the sites moves to three conffiles in
    /etc/nginx/snippets/: keel-web-site.conf, keel-web-front.conf and
    keel-web-acme.conf. The postrm removes http-mode's links.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 06:00:00 +0000

keel-web (0.3.6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The manifest declares state.replicate with
    /var/www/keel-default (handbook decision 0068). On a database
    pair Syncthing replicates the directory, so the site content
    the operator or an application layer puts there reaches the
    other node. An application that inherits web adds its own paths
    beside this one.

